# G051 — Big Integer

*v2. See `big-integer.md` for prose: unbounded-precision arithmetic,
value class whose entire point is invariants the language can't guarantee.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Cumulative Token Audit Across All Conversations

[token-audit
    IN ORDER TO
        sum token counts across a year of conversations
        using arbitrary-precision arithmetic that cannot silently overflow
        and cross-check against the per-day aggregate

    @conversations <- [db[conversations]]
    @total <- @conversations
        folded through [bigint[add {x: @acc,
                                     y: [bigint[from-int {n: @conv:token-count}]]}]]
        starting from [bigint[zero]]

    @daily-total <- @daily-totals
        folded through [bigint[add {x: @acc, y: @day-total}]]
        starting from [bigint[zero]]

    @total
        where
            equals @daily-total (two aggregations must agree)
            and is not negative
        || -> #audit-mismatch
            actual: [bigint[to-string {x: @total}]]
            expected: [bigint[to-string {x: @daily-total}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[bigint
    zero
    from-int    {n}
    parse       {s}
    to-string   {x}
    neg         {x}
    abs         {x}
    add         {x, y}
    sub         {x, y}
    mul         {x, y}
    cmp         {x, y}          ;; -> -1 | 0 | 1
    equal       {x, y}
    negative    {x}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: overflow impossibility as `where` assertion

BigInt's entire point is that overflow is not a concern. In Innate
this shows up as a `where` clause the resolver must satisfy:

[bigint[add {x: @a, y: @b}]
    where result precision grows to accommodate any sum
        and no silent truncation or wraparound occurs
        and result equals x + y in the integers
]

Any resolver claiming to implement `bigint` that violates this contract
is non-conformant; callers can rely on the invariant.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @z <- [bigint[zero]]
    @n <- [bigint[from-int {n: 123456789}]]
    @m <- [bigint[parse {s: "987654321098765432109876543210"}]]

    @sum <- [bigint[add {x: @n, y: @m}]]
    -> [bigint[to-string {x: @sum}]]
    ;; -> "987654321098765432233333332999"   (no overflow)

    -> [bigint[cmp {x: @sum, y: @m}]]        ;; -> 1 (sum > m)
    -> [bigint[equal {x: @z, y: [bigint[zero]]}]]   ;; -> true
]
